hannels</article-title><source>Neuron</source><year>1996</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>113</fpage><lpage>122</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8562074</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="FN1"><label>1</label><p><italic>Abbreviations used in this paper:</italic> GHK, Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz; I-V,  current-voltage; i-V, single-channel current-voltage; [Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup>]<sub>o</sub>, external  Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> concentration; <italic>P</italic><sub>open</sub>, peak open probability; P-V, <italic>P</italic><sub>open</sub>-voltage;  NMG, <italic>N</italic>-methyl-<sc>d</sc>-glucamine; WT, wild type.</p></fn><fn id="FN2"><label>2</label><p>Note that all substitutes for Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> in this paper are predominantly  monovalent cations at neutral pH.</p></fn><fn id="FN4"><label>4</label><p>Cs<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> was used as a Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> substitute in this experiment to avoid effects  on slow inactivation (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="B35">Townsend and Horn, 1997</xref>).</p></fn><fn id="FN3"><label>3</label><p>K<sup>&#x0002b;</sup> was measurably permeant in these experiments, however, with a  permeability ratio of 0.08 compared with Na<sup>&#x0002b;</sup>.</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>